@cc [owner:PopDaph,label:architecture] dfm-pure
Production files in this directory MUST NOT import React, DOM APIs, Node built-ins, `lib/api/*`,
database or Redis clients, or anything transitively depending on them. The codec runs in the
browser inside the editor host and on the server inside agent tools, and both must load the same
code. `*.test.ts` and `*.test_utils.ts` files are exempt.

@cc [owner:PopDaph,label:coding] dfm-grammar-once
Each Dust directive's pattern, attribute schema and builder MUST be defined exactly once, in
the module that parses it: `anchors.ts` for the body markers, `annotations.ts` for the block
directives. The fences, the directive-line pattern and the attribute tokenizer live in
`grammar.ts`; plain Markdown questions are asked of the parser in `parser.ts`. No other
production file MAY spell such a literal. Tests and fixtures write the format literally on
purpose.

@cc [owner:PopDaph,label:testing] dfm-fixtures-are-canonical
Every file in `tests/fixtures/` MUST be reproduced byte for byte by `serializeDfm` after
`parseDfm`, and `tests/document.test.ts` MUST check every file in that directory. A change
to the grammar MUST update the fixtures and the format section of README.md in the same change.
A new directive gets its own fixture file rather than extending an existing one.

@cc [owner:PopDaph,label:performance] dfm-bounded-input
Every public function MUST refuse text outside `INPUT_LIMITS` (length, leading container run
per line, inline delimiter count, list item count) with a located error before any of its
parser calls, checking the exact text each call receives: the lines after the front matter in
`parseDfm`, the body, each comment message and the serialized source in `serializeDfm`, and in
`anchorComment` the body, the anchor-free text and the anchored result. No input can then hold
the parser for more than about a second per parse. `tests/bounds.test.ts` MUST check this
against a spied parser for every public function and every bound. Callers on a request path
MUST NOT rely on these bounds for latency and SHOULD isolate the codec (a worker or a timeout)
once it is reachable from untrusted input.
